{"task_id": "Mbpp/141", "solution": "from typing import List, TypeVar\nT = TypeVar('T')\ndef pancake_sort(arr: List[T]) -> List[T]:\n    \"\"\"Sort a list using the pancake sorting algorithm.\n\n    The function returns a new list containing the elements of `arr`\n    sorted in ascending order. The original list remains unchanged.\n\n    Parameters\n    ----------\n    arr : List[T]\n        The list to be sorted. Elements must support comparison operators\n        (<, >).\n\n    Returns\n    -------\n    List[T]\n        A new list with the elements sorted in ascending order.\n\n    Raises\n    ------\n    TypeError\n        If `arr` is not a list or if elements cannot be compared.\n    \"\"\"\n    if not isinstance(arr, list):\n        raise TypeError(\"pancake_sort expects a list as input\")\n\n    n = len(arr)\n    result = list(arr)  # Shallow copy to preserve original\n\n    # Iterate over sublist sizes from n down to 2\n    for curr_size in range(n, 1, -1):\n        # Find index of the maximum element in result[0:curr_size]\n        try:\n            max_index = max(range(curr_size), key=result.__getitem__)\n        except TypeError as exc:\n            raise TypeError(\"elements must be comparable\") from exc\n\n        # If max already at correct position, skip\n        if max_index == curr_size - 1:\n            continue\n\n        # Flip maximum element to front if it's not already there\n        if max_index != 0:\n            result[:max_index + 1] = reversed(result[:max_index + 1])\n\n        # Flip it to its final position at curr_size - 1\n        result[:curr_size] = reversed(result[:curr_size])\n\n    return result"}
{"task_id": "Mbpp/142", "solution": "def count_samepair(seq1, seq2, seq3):\n    \"\"\"Count positions where all three sequences have the same element.\n\n    Parameters\n    ----------\n    seq1, seq2, seq3 : list or tuple\n        Sequences to compare. Only the first min(len(seq1), len(seq2), len(seq3)) elements are considered.\n\n    Returns\n    -------\n    int\n        Number of positions where all three elements are equal.\n\n    Raises\n    ------\n    TypeError\n        If any argument is not a list or tuple.\n\n    Examples\n    --------\n    >>> count_samepair([1, 2, 3], [1, 2, 3], [1, 2, 3])\n    3\n    >>> count_samepair([1, 2, 3], [1, 4, 3], [1, 5, 3])\n    2\n    \"\"\"\n    if not all(isinstance(s, (list, tuple)) for s in (seq1, seq2, seq3)):\n        raise TypeError(\"All arguments must be list or tuple\")\n\n    min_len = min(len(seq1), len(seq2), len(seq3))\n    count = 0\n\n    for i in range(min_len):\n        if seq1[i] == seq2[i] == seq3[i]:\n            count += 1\n\n    return count"}
