{"task_id": "Mbpp/752", "solution": "def jacobsthal(n: int) -> int:\n    \"\"\"\n    Computes the nth Jacobsthal number where the sequence is defined as J(0)=0,\n    J(1)=1, and for n>=2, J(n)=J(n-1)+2*J(n-2). Returns an integer value.\n\n    Parameters\n    ----------\n    n : int\n        The index of the Jacobsthal number to compute. Must be a non-negative integer.\n\n    Returns\n    -------\n    int\n        The nth Jacobsthal number.\n\n    Raises\n    ------\n    TypeError\n        If n is not an integer.\n    ValueError\n        If n is negative.\n\n    Examples\n    --------\n    >>> jacobsthal(0)\n    0\n    >>> jacobsthal(1)\n    1\n    >>> jacobsthal(2)\n    1\n    >>> jacobsthal(3)\n    3\n    >>> jacobsthal(10)\n    121\n    \"\"\"\n    if not isinstance(n, int):\n        raise TypeError(\"n must be an integer\")\n    if n < 0:\n        raise ValueError(\"n must be non-negative\")\n\n    if n == 0:\n        return 0\n    if n == 1:\n        return 1\n\n    prev, curr = 0, 1\n    for _ in range(2, n + 1):\n        next_val = curr + 2 * prev\n        prev, curr = curr, next_val\n\n    return curr\n\n__all__ = [\"jacobsthal\"]"}
{"task_id": "Mbpp/753", "solution": "from typing import List, Tuple\ndef min_k(records: List[Tuple[str, int]], k: int) -> List[Tuple[str, int]]:\n    \"\"\"Return the k tuples with the smallest second elements.\n\n    The result is sorted ascending by second element and preserves\n    original order among tuples with equal second values.\n\n    Parameters\n    ----------\n    records : List[Tuple[str, int]]\n        Input list of (string, int) tuples.\n    k : int\n        Number of smallest tuples to return.\n\n    Returns\n    -------\n    List[Tuple[str, int]]\n        The k tuples with smallest second elements.\n    \"\"\"\n    if k <= 0:\n        return []\n\n    n = len(records)\n    if k >= n:\n        k = n\n\n    sorted_records = sorted(records, key=lambda t: t[1])\n    return sorted_records[:k]"}
{"task_id": "Mbpp/754", "solution": "def extract_index_list(seq1, seq2, seq3):\n    \"\"\"\n    Return a list of elements that are identical across three sequences at the same index.\n\n    Parameters\n    ----------\n    seq1, seq2, seq3 : sequence\n        Any objects supporting ``__len__`` and ``__getitem__``.  The function\n        accepts lists, tuples, ranges, arrays, or any custom sequence types.\n\n    Returns\n    -------\n    list\n        Elements where ``seq1[i] == seq2[i] == seq3[i]``.  The order of the\n        returned elements matches their order in the input sequences.\n\n    Raises\n    ------\n    TypeError\n        If any argument does not support ``__len__`` or ``__getitem__``.\n        Propagated exceptions from element comparison (e.g. unorderable types)\n        are allowed to surface to the caller.\n\n    Examples\n    --------\n    >>> extract_index_list([1, 2, 3], [1, 4, 3], [1, 5, 3])\n    [1, 3]\n    >>> extract_index_list([], [], [])\n    []\n    >>> extract_index_list(range(5), range(5), range(5))\n    [0, 1, 2, 3, 4]\n    \"\"\"\n    # Validation\n    for seq, name in ((seq1, \"seq1\"), (seq2, \"seq2\"), (seq3, \"seq3\")):\n        if not hasattr(seq, \"__len__\") or not hasattr(seq, \"__getitem__\"):\n            raise TypeError(f\"{name} must be a sequence type\")\n\n    limit = min(len(seq1), len(seq2), len(seq3))\n    result = []\n\n    for i in range(limit):\n        if seq1[i] == seq2[i] == seq3[i]:\n            result.append(seq1[i])\n\n    return result"}
